===== ../content/playground/destructive-demo.md =====
intro:
  prose "This runbook exists to show the acknowledgement gate. If you can read this without a dialog first, something is wrong."
step: "Confirm the target" section=false
  prose "Check you are pointing at the replica you meant to rebuild."
  code lang="bash" label="Check the target" body="mysql -h db-replica -e \"SELECT @@hostname, @@read_only;\""
step: "Rebuild the replica" section=false
  prose "The destructive step, reached only after the acknowledgement."
  code lang="bash" label="Rebuild" body="mysql -h db-replica -e \"STOP REPLICA; RESET REPLICA ALL;\""
===== ../content/playground/gallery.md =====
intro:
  prose "This opening paragraph is intro prose before the first step. It can carry **bold**, *italic*, `inline code`, and a [link](https://example.com) — all inline-markdown forms."
step: "Prose, lists and tables" section=false
  prose "A step body can hold plain prose, bullet lists and tables. The prose here is deliberately long enough to wrap a few lines, so line-height and measure are visible."
  list ordered=false
    item "First bullet — plain text."
    item "Second bullet with `inline code` and a **bold** run."
    item "Third bullet — a longer one that wraps onto a second line so spacing between items is visible."
    item "A bullet that contains a nested list:"
    child ordered=false
      item "Nested bullet one."
      item "Nested bullet two with `inline code`."
  table headers=["Column" "Meaning" "Example"]
    row ["`HOST`" "Target host" "`db-2.prod.internal`"]
    row ["`PROCESSLIST_ID`" "Blocked worker" "`4123`"]
    row ["Status" "Whether a step is done" "`done` / `pending`"]
  heading "A subheading inside a step"
  prose "Subheadings split a long step without starting a new numbered card, as above."
step: "Code blocks" section=false
  prose "Code blocks take a language and an optional bracketed label; the label renders a header with a per-block done checkbox. Variables substituted from the panel are highlighted."
  code lang="bash" label="Promote the replica" body="export HOST=\"{{HOST}}\"\nmysql -h \"$HOST\" -e \"STOP SLAVE; RESET SLAVE ALL;\""
  code lang="sql" label="Inspect the worker" body="SELECT id, time, state FROM information_schema.processlist WHERE id = {{PROCESSLIST_ID}};"
  code lang="text" label="A label-less fence" body="Any lower-case language tag is accepted; this fence has no bracketed label."
  code lang="bash" label="Uses the secret" body="curl -H \"Authorization: Bearer {{API_TOKEN}}\" \"https://{{HOST}}/healthz\""
step: "Notices and branch" section=false
  prose "All three notice variants, then a decision callout."
  notice variant="info" message="This is an info notice — context, links and read-only remarks."
  notice variant="warn" message="This is a warning — a step that briefly stops writes, or a value to double-check."
  notice variant="danger" message="This is a danger notice — destructive. READ EVERY STEP BEFORE DOING ANYTHING."
  branch "- If the replica's IO thread is running, continue to the next step.\n- If it has stopped, rebuild from the nightly snapshot instead.\n- If neither, page the on-call DBA."
rollback: "Roll back the promotion" section=false
  prose "Rollback steps render after `---rollback`, in the danger styling and without a number."
  code lang="bash" label="Demote the node" body="mysql -h \"{{HOST}}\" -e \"SET GLOBAL read_only = ON;\""
rollback: "Confirm the rollback held" section=false
  prose "A second rollback step, so the spacing between consecutive danger cards is rendered and reviewable."
===== ../content/playground/writing-runbooks.md =====
intro:
  prose "This page is a runbook about writing runbooks. Read it, copy the patterns, then delete it."
step: "Where runbooks live" section=false
  prose "The filesystem is the taxonomy — there is no database and no CMS. A runbook is a Markdown file with YAML frontmatter:"
  code lang="text" label="Content layout" body="content/<system>/<category>/<file>.md"
  list ordered=false
    item "`<system>` becomes a top-level sidebar group (the directory name, title-cased)."
    item "`<category>` becomes a subheading under the system."
    item "A file directly under the system (`content/<system>/<file>.md`) has no subheading."
    item "The URL is `/<slug>`, and `slug` comes from frontmatter — not the filename."
  prose "Directory names map to display names and sidebar order in `parser/parser.go` (`systemLabels`, `systemOrder`, `categoryOrder`). Within a category, runbooks sort by the optional `order:` field, then by title. Content is read from disk at runtime, so edits need no rebuild — just refresh."
step: "Frontmatter" section=false
  prose "Only `title` and `slug` are required; everything else is optional."
  table headers=["Field" "What it does"]
    row ["`title`" "Sidebar label and `<h1>`."]
    row ["`slug`" "The URL, `/<slug>`."]
    row ["`order`" "Position within its category (lower first; absent sorts last)."]
    row ["`description`" "One-line summary under the `<h1>`."]
    row ["`symptoms`" "Extra keywords the index search matches."]
    row ["`common`" "`true` pins it into the \"Common issues\" shortlist."]
    row ["`notice`" "A passive banner above the body."]
    row ["`acknowledge`" "Marks the runbook destructive: the page is gated behind an \"I understand\" dialog until accepted (once per session)."]
    row ["`vars`" "Runtime inputs — see the next step."]
step: "Inputs with `vars`" section=false
  prose "`vars` render a panel of inputs. Each value substitutes into code blocks as a `{{TOKEN}}`, where the token is uppercase A–Z and underscore only:"
  code lang="bash" label="Use the inputs" body="ssh \"{{HOST}}\"\nsystemctl --user restart \"{{SERVICE}}\""
  list ordered=false
    item "Give the diagnostic lookup to a `hint` rather than a literal `<placeholder>` in the code — angle brackets inside a fence look like real syntax."
    item "Set `secret: true` to render a password field."
    item "Substitution happens in the browser, so values **never leave the page**."
step: "Steps, subheadings and prose" section=false
  prose "`## Heading` starts a numbered step. `### Heading` splits a long step without starting a new card. A step body takes ordinary Markdown: **bold**, *italic*, `inline code`, [links](https://example.com), bullet lists and tables."
  notice variant="info" message="Titles lead with the symptom, not the mechanism — a runbook is read under duress."
  prose "For a destructive runbook, don't rely on a banner: add `acknowledge:` and the reader must accept it before the page is usable."
  code lang="yaml" label="Frontmatter" body="acknowledge: \"This drops and rebuilds the replica. Take it out of the load balancer first.\""
step: "Code blocks and checkoffs" section=false
  prose "A fenced block takes a lowercase language tag and an optional bracketed label. The label renders a header with a per-block **done** checkbox, and a step ticks itself once all its blocks are ticked."
  code lang="sql" label="Inspect the target" body="SELECT id, state FROM information_schema.processlist WHERE user = '{{SERVICE}}';"
step: "Notices and decisions" section=false
  prose "Three notice variants carry different weight:"
  notice variant="info" message="Context, links and read-only remarks."
  notice variant="warn" message="A step that briefly stops writes, or a value to double-check."
  notice variant="danger" message="Destructive. READ EVERY STEP BEFORE DOING ANYTHING."
  prose "A branch callout lists the decisions a reader might make:"
  branch "- If the check passed, continue.\n- If it failed, rebuild instead.\n- If neither, page the on-call."
step: "Rollback" section=false
  prose "`---rollback` on its own line closes the numbered steps and starts a separate, danger-styled, unnumbered section. This page ends with one, so you can see the styling:"
  code lang="text" label="The marker" body="---rollback"
  prose "That is the whole format — delete this file, and any other content you don't need, from `content/` when you are done."
rollback: "Undo the example restart" section=false
  prose "Rollback steps are unnumbered and danger-styled. A real runbook puts the actual undo commands here."
  code lang="bash" label="Stop the example service" body="systemctl --user stop \"{{SERVICE}}\""
===== ../docs/get-started/install.md =====
step: "Run the container" section=true
  prose "The app ships as a container image and carries no content. You mount a directory of Markdown runbooks."
  code lang="bash" label="Start Runbooks" body="docker run -d --name runbooks \\\n  -p 8090:8090 \\\n  -v \"$PWD/content:/app/content\" \\\n  -v runbooks-data:/app/data \\\n  ghcr.io/runbooks-help/runbooks:latest"
  prose "Open [localhost:8090](http://localhost:8090)."
  list ordered=false
    item "`/app/content` is the content root (`CONTENT_DIR`). If it is empty the index renders a welcome page; add a `.md` file to populate it."
    item "`/app/data` holds the SQLite identity database when identity is on. It is unused otherwise, but mount it so a later upgrade keeps its data."
    item "The container runs as a non-root user and exposes `/healthz`."
  prose "Pin a version tag (`:vX.Y.Z`) rather than `:latest` for a production deployment."
step: "Your first runbook" section=true
  prose "Create `content/mysql/backup/verify-backup.md`:"
  code lang="markdown" label="" body="---\ntitle: Verify the nightly backup\nslug: verify-backup\ndescription: Check that last night's dump exists and restores.\n---\n\n## Check the dump\n\nThe dump should be from tonight and non-trivial in size."
  prose "The path puts it under **MySQL › Backup** in the sidebar, and the URL is `/verify-backup` (from the slug, not the filename). Content is read from disk at startup: no rebuild, no restart. Add a code block, a notice or `vars` as you need them: see [Writing a runbook](../guides/writing-runbooks.md)."
step: "Configuration" section=true
  prose "Every setting is an environment variable; the full list is [Configuration](../reference/configuration.md). The common ones:"
  table headers=["Var" "Default" "Meaning"]
    row ["`PORT`" "`8090`" "HTTP listen port."]
    row ["`CONTENT_DIR`" "`content`" "Directory the runbooks are read from."]
    row ["`PUBLIC_URL`" "_(unset)_" "Absolute site base; enables canonical/OpenGraph tags and `/sitemap.xml`."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_DB_DRIVER`" "_(unset)_" "`sqlite`, `mysql` or `postgres`; unset leaves the app public."]
step: "Build from source" section=true
  prose "The image is the supported distribution. The FSL permits building from source:"
  code lang="bash" label="Build and run" body="mise install\nmise run build\n./runbooks            # serves on :8090, reads ./content"
step: "Where next" section=true
  list ordered=false
    item "[Writing a runbook](../guides/writing-runbooks.md)"
    item "[Deployment](../operations/deployment.md)"
    item "[Identity](../operations/identity.md)"
===== ../docs/get-started/overview.md =====
intro:
  prose "Runbooks is a small, self-hosted web app that turns a directory of Markdown files into interactive, step-by-step operational runbooks. **Prod broke? Runbooks help.**"
step: "What it is" section=true
  list ordered=false
    item "**Files are the content.** A runbook is a Markdown file with YAML frontmatter. The directory path decides the sidebar. There is no database, no CMS and no authoring UI: you edit files in your editor and commit them."
    item "**One binary, one container.** The app reads a content directory at runtime, so content changes need no rebuild and no redeploy."
    item "**Procedures and documentation in one tool.** A `##` heading renders as a numbered, tickable, collapsible **step** (a procedure) or an unnumbered **section** (reference prose), and one page can mix both."
    item "**Built for reading under duress.** Symptom-first titles, collapsible steps, code blocks with a per-block \"done\" box, decision callouts, a rollback section, inline variables, notes, and a Zen mode that shows one step at a time."
step: "The mental model" section=true
  table headers=["Thing" "How it works"]
    row ["Taxonomy" "`content/<system>/<category>/<file>.md` → sidebar groups and subheadings"]
    row ["URL" "the frontmatter `slug`, not the filename: `/<slug>`"]
    row ["Ordering" "`_meta.yml` at the content root, then per-file `order:`"]
    row ["Steps vs sections" "a `##` heading; `layout: sections` or `---sections` / `---steps`"]
    row ["Inputs" "frontmatter `vars`, substituted into code as `{{NAME}}`, client-side only"]
    row ["Search" "in-memory, full-body, served at `/api/runbooks/v1/search`"]
    row ["Agents" "`/llms.txt`, `/<slug>.md` (raw Markdown), and the search API"]
step: "Identity is optional" section=true
  prose "By default the app is public: no accounts, no database. Turn on passkey (WebAuthn) identity, or delegate to an upstream proxy/SSO gateway, to gate reads and attribute writes to named users. It is off until you configure it, and when it is on the instance is private (crawlers are disallowed)."
step: "Who it is for" section=true
  prose "Solo operators, small teams and studios who keep their operational knowledge in files and want it readable in the moment. It is not aimed at large enterprises: there is no procurement process, no on-prem SSO tier and no enterprise sales motion."
step: "Licence" section=true
  prose "Runbooks is **source-available** under FSL-1.1-MIT: free to self-host, modify and use commercially, with no competing-use right, and each release converts to MIT two years after publication."
step: "Where next" section=true
  list ordered=false
    item "[Install & quickstart](install.md)"
    item "[Writing a runbook](../guides/writing-runbooks.md)"
===== ../docs/guides/writing-runbooks.md =====
intro:
  prose "A runbook is a Markdown file with YAML frontmatter, under the content root. The file path and the frontmatter decide where it appears and at what URL."
step: "Where files live" section=true
  prose "`content/<system>/<category>/<file>.md`:"
  code lang="text" label="Content layout" body="content/\n  mysql/\n    replication/\n      mts-deadlock-recovery.md   → MySQL ▸ Replication\n  kubernetes/\n    networking/\n      k3s-log-proxy-502.md"
  list ordered=false
    item "`<system>` is a top-level sidebar group (the directory name, title-cased)."
    item "`<category>` is a subheading under the system."
    item "A file directly in `content/<system>/` renders under the system with no subheading."
  prose "Display names and order live in `content/_meta.yml` at the top of the tree: an ordered list where position is the order:"
  code lang="yaml" label="" body="- mysql:\n    title: MySQL\n    categories: [replication, failover, backup]\n- kubernetes\n- backend"
  prose "An item is a bare directory name or `name: {title, categories}`; a category may carry a title the same way. Unlisted directories are appended after the listed ones, alphabetically. Within a category, runbooks sort by the optional frontmatter `order:` (lower first; absent sorts last), then by title. Use `order:` to pull the most likely on-call runbook to the top of its category."
step: "Frontmatter" section=true
  prose "Only `title` and `slug` are required; everything else is optional."
  code lang="yaml" label="" body="---\ntitle: Replication Lag (MTS Deadlock)\nslug: mts-deadlock-recovery\norder: 1\ndescription: One-line summary under the title and on index cards.\nsymptoms:\n  - Last_SQL_Error\n  - duplicate entry error 1062\ncommon: true\nnotice: \"READ EVERY STEP BEFORE DOING ANYTHING!\"\nacknowledge: \"This drops and rebuilds the replica.\"\nvars:\n  - id: some-ip\n    label: Server IP\n    var: SERVER_IP\n    placeholder: \"e.g. 10.0.0.5\"\n    hint: How to find this value (plain-text popover on hover)\n  - id: db-password\n    label: DB password\n    var: DB_PASS\n    secret: true\n---"
  table headers=["Field" "What it does"]
    row ["`title`" "Sidebar label and `<h1>`."]
    row ["`slug`" "The URL, `/<slug>`, **not** the filename."]
    row ["`order`" "Position within its category (lower first)."]
    row ["`description`" "One-line summary under the `<h1>` and on index cards."]
    row ["`symptoms`" "Extra keywords the index filter and search match."]
    row ["`common`" "`true` pins it into the \"Common issues\" shortlist."]
    row ["`notice`" "A passive banner above the body."]
    row ["`acknowledge`" "Marks the runbook destructive: the page is gated behind an \"I understand\" dialog until accepted (once per session)."]
    row ["`vars`" "Runtime inputs; see below."]
  prose "Keep `symptoms:` on every runbook: the index matches title, description, symptoms and the system/category names, and the body search reaches through the file."
step: "Inputs with `vars`" section=true
  prose "`vars` render a panel of inputs. Each value substitutes into code blocks as a `{{TOKEN}}`, where the token is uppercase A–Z and underscore only:"
  code lang="bash" label="Use the inputs" body="ssh \"{{SERVER_IP}}\"\nsystemctl restart \"{{SERVICE}}\""
  list ordered=false
    item "Give the diagnostic lookup to a `hint` rather than a literal `<placeholder>` inside the code: angle brackets in a fence look like real syntax."
    item "Set `secret: true` to render a password field."
    item "Substitution happens in the browser, so values **never leave the page**."
step: "Steps and sections" section=true
  prose "A `##` heading is a **step** by default: numbered, tickable and collapsible. Add `layout: sections` to the frontmatter to render headings as **sections** instead (anchored, unnumbered, always open), or switch mid-page with `---sections` / `---steps` on their own line. The GitHub-safe comment forms (`<!-- sections -->` / `<!-- steps -->`) do the same and stay invisible in a rendered Markdown view (GitHub, any CommonMark renderer)."
  prose "The two mix freely: a page can explain, hand over a numbered procedure, then continue explaining. Numbering counts steps only, the Contents list and prev/next do not care which a heading is, and `###` subheadings are anchored too."
step: "Resolve the lag" section=false
  prose "The numbered steps from here to the next separator are a procedure, with the same tick boxes and collapse behaviour as every other step:"
  list ordered=true
    item "Confirm the lag on the replica."
    item "Stop the SQL thread."
    item "Clear the deadlock and restart the thread."
step: "Code blocks" section=true
  prose "Add a language and an optional bracketed label; the label renders a header with a per-block \"done\" checkbox. The language tag must be lowercase letters:"
  code lang="sql" label="Run on mysql-prod-primary" body="SELECT @@read_only, @@global.gtid_executed;"
step: "Notices" section=true
  code lang="markdown" label="" body="> [!info] Informational note. [!warn] Something to be careful about. [!danger] This causes an outage."
  prose "The GitHub alert keywords (`NOTE`, `TIP`, `IMPORTANT`, `WARNING`, `CAUTION`) are accepted and mapped onto info/warn/danger, and the marker is case-insensitive."
step: "Decision points" section=true
  prose "A branch block presents the choices at a fork in the procedure:"
  code lang="markdown" label="" body="> [!branch]\n>\n> - All good → [Next step](#next-step)\n> - Something broke → [Roll back](#rollback)"
step: "Rollback" section=true
  prose "`---rollback` (or `<!-- rollback -->`) on its own line starts a separate rollback section: danger-styled, unnumbered, and read as \"what to do if it broke\". Everything after it is rollback steps."
step: "Glossary" section=true
  prose "One file at the content root, `content/_glossary.yml`, defines the shared terms for the instance. A configured term highlights wherever prose renders (step and section titles, notices, lists, tables) and shows its expansion, detail and optional link on hover or focus. Code is never touched."
  code lang="yaml" label="" body="- term: MTS\n  expansion: Multi-Threaded Replica\n  description: MySQL replica applying transactions in parallel workers.\n  link: https://internal/glossary#mts"
  prose "`term` and `expansion` are required; a missing one fails startup. Matching is exact, case-sensitive and whole-word, so `IT` never matches \"it\"."
step: "Conventions" section=true
  list ordered=false
    item "**Titles lead with the symptom, not the mechanism**: \"Replication Lag (MTS Deadlock)\", not \"MTS Deadlock Recovery\". This is a runbook read under duress."
    item "**One concern per step.** If a step needs a sub-decision, use `###` or a branch block rather than burying it in prose."
    item "**Destructive procedures declare `acknowledge:`** so the reader must confirm before the page is usable; `notice:` stays a passive banner."
step: "Where next" section=true
  list ordered=false
    item "[Configuration reference](../reference/configuration.md)"
    item "[Deployment](../operations/deployment.md)"
    item "[Agent access](../operations/agent-access.md)"
===== ../docs/operations/agent-access.md =====
intro:
  prose "Runbooks are plain Markdown with YAML frontmatter, which makes them easy for an LLM or an automation script to read. On a hosted instance they are also easy to expose through a small read-only surface rather than scraping the rendered HTML."
step: "The simplest path: clone the content directory" section=true
  prose "For a local agent, the best route needs no API: **clone the repo (or the `content/` directory) and point the agent at `content/`.** It gets the raw source (frontmatter included) and can grep across runbooks, follow cross-references, and read the git history for when a step changed and why. No network, no credentials, no parsed HTML."
  prose "`content/` mirrors the app: `content/<system>/<category>/<file>.md`, the slug in frontmatter, and the sidebar order in `content/_meta.yml`. An agent can read `_meta.yml` and each runbook's frontmatter directly."
  prose "There is deliberately **no `AGENTS.md` inside `content/`**: the parser treats every `*.md` under `content/` as a runbook, and a file without frontmatter is a startup error. Agent guidance lives here and in the repo's `AGENTS.md`."
step: "The hosted read API" section=true
  prose "When the agent runs remotely (a bot, a hosted chat, a different trust domain), it cannot clone anything. Instead the app exposes a **read-only** surface:"
  table headers=["Endpoint" "What it returns"]
    row ["`GET /llms.txt`" "A generated index: every runbook grouped by system, each linking to its `.md`."]
    row ["`GET /<slug>.md`" "One runbook's raw Markdown, frontmatter included."]
    row ["`GET /api/runbooks/v1/search?q=…&limit=…`" "Body search across titles, symptoms, steps and code; JSON with snippets and step anchors."]
  prose "With identity **off** these are public, like the rest of the app. With identity **on** they require either a session or a **read-scoped API key**, sent as:"
  code lang="" label="" body="Authorization: Bearer rbk_…"
step: "Creating an API key" section=true
  prose "Keys are minted from your own account page and are read-only by construction."
step: "Create the key" section=false
  prose "On an identity-enabled instance, open `/account` → **API keys**, give the key a label and choose **Create key**."
step: "Copy it once" section=false
  prose "The raw key (`rbk_…`) is shown **once**: copy it then; only its SHA-256 hash is stored and it cannot be recovered. Revoke a key from the same table."
step: "What a key can do" section=true
  prose "A key is **read-only by construction**: it can call the three endpoints above and nothing else. It cannot view the HTML pages, reach the admin area, use git sync, or acknowledge a destructive runbook. A revoked key, or one whose owner has been disabled, stops working on the next request."
===== ../docs/operations/deployment.md =====
step: "Storage" section=true
  list ordered=false
    item "**Runbooks** are plain markdown read from a content **source** at runtime; not stored in a database, and not part of a backup of it. By default that is a local directory (`CONTENT_DIR`, default `content`); set `CONTENT_SOURCE=git` to read from a repository instead, cloned into `CONTENT_GIT_CACHE` (records under `GITSYNC_BASE_PATH` are skipped). The image ships an empty directory (it never bundles content); mount yours there, or point the source wherever you like."
    item "**Identity** (users, passkeys, sessions, invites, audit events) lives in the configured database. SQLite is the default and keeps everything in one file at `file:./data/runbooks.db`."
    item "In the container the working directory is `/app`, so the SQLite file is `/app/data/runbooks.db`. Mount a volume at `/app/data` to persist it."
    item "The schema is applied at startup from embedded, idempotent migrations. There is no external migration tool and no migration step: **to upgrade, replace the binary or image and restart.**"
    item "`IDENTITY_PUBLIC_URL` is effectively immutable once passkeys exist: changing the host invalidates every credential."
step: "Content" section=true
  prose "Runbooks are read from a **source**, selected by `CONTENT_SOURCE`:"
  list ordered=false
    item "`local` (default): read `CONTENT_DIR` directly. Mount your content there."
    item "`git`: clone `GITSYNC_REPO` (branch `GITSYNC_BRANCH`) into `CONTENT_GIT_CACHE` and read `CONTENT_GIT_PATH` inside it. Content sits at the repo root; the notes records path (`GITSYNC_BASE_PATH`) is skipped by the content walk."
  heading "SSH credentials"
  prose "SSH remotes authenticate with the **ambient SSH agent** by default: with no `GITSYNC_SSH_KEY` and no `GITSYNC_TOKEN`, `go-git` connects through `$SSH_AUTH_SOCK`, so a running `ssh-agent`, 1Password, or equivalent is enough on a workstation; there is nothing to configure. Host keys are verified against `SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS`, then `~/.ssh/known_hosts` / `/etc/ssh/ssh_known_hosts`; there is no `accept-new`, so the host must already be known."
  prose "A **deployment or CI job has no agent**, so supply the key explicitly. Mount the private key and a `known_hosts` file read-only, then point the variables at them:"
  code lang="bash" label="" body="docker run -d \\\n  -e GITSYNC_REPO=git@github.com:org/notes.git \\\n  -e GITSYNC_SSH_KEY=/run/secrets/gitsync_key \\\n  -e SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS=/run/secrets/known_hosts \\\n  -v /host/gitsync_key:/run/secrets/gitsync_key:ro \\\n  -v /host/known_hosts:/run/secrets/known_hosts:ro \\\n  ghcr.io/runbooks-help/runbooks:<tag>"
  prose "The key is read as a file path, not a value, so an orchestrator secret mounted as a file works directly. HTTPS remotes need `GITSYNC_TOKEN` instead and no agent."
  heading "Startup order"
  list ordered=false
    item "**Cache present:** the cached checkout is parsed and served immediately, then the remote is fetched in the background. An unreachable remote does not delay or fail boot; the last successful fetch keeps serving."
    item "**No cache:** the clone happens before the server listens. Boot fails only when there is neither a cache nor a successful fetch."
  prose "A failed fetch or a parse error keeps the current in-memory snapshot (last-good); it never serves a half-loaded set."
  heading "Refreshing content"
  prose "`POST /api/content/v1/refresh` fetches the source, parses it and swaps the snapshot atomically, so no request observes a partially-loaded set and in-flight requests keep the set they started on."
  list ordered=false
    item "With identity on it is **admin-only**: a signed-in admin; an API key is read-only by construction and cannot call it."
    item "With identity off it requires `Authorization: Bearer $CONTENT_REFRESH_TOKEN`, and is disabled (`403`) when no token is configured."
  prose "A local source is re-read in place; only a git source fetches."
  prose "Set `CONTENT_REFRESH_INTERVAL` (e.g. `5m`) to refresh on a timer instead of relying on an external scheduler. It is opt-in and requires at least `1m`; each wait is jittered ±10% so several instances polling one repo do not beat in lockstep. Refreshes are serialized (a tick that lands while a refresh is already running is skipped, and the manual endpoint waits its turn), and a failed refresh only logs and keeps the last-good snapshot. There is no webhook."
  heading "Cache"
  prose "`CONTENT_GIT_CACHE` (default `data/content`) is a single inspectable directory under the data volume, and is an ordinary git clone. It is not a backed-up artifact: a restart with the remote reachable re-fetches, and an empty cache just means a fresh clone at boot."
step: "Backing up SQLite" section=true
  prose "Identity lives in the configured database (SQLite by default) and can be backed up while the app runs."
step: "Take a snapshot" section=false
  prose "`VACUUM INTO` writes a consistent, self-contained copy:"
  code lang="bash" label="" body="sqlite3 ./data/runbooks.db \"VACUUM INTO '/backup/runbooks-$(date +%F).db'\""
step: "Restore it" section=false
  prose "Stop the app, point `IDENTITY_DB_DSN` at the copy, and restart:"
  code lang="bash" label="" body="IDENTITY_DB_DSN=file:/backup/runbooks-2026-10-04.db ./runbooks"
  prose "A plain file copy of `data/runbooks.db` is equally valid with the app stopped. Verified once against this procedure: create a database, `VACUUM INTO` a copy, open the copy and read a row back; the snapshot restores cleanly."
step: "MySQL and PostgreSQL" section=true
  prose "Use the database's own tooling: `mysqldump` / `pg_dump`, or the managed service's snapshots. Runbooks ships no backup code. The identity tables are:"
  code lang="" label="" body="users, credentials, sessions, invites, webauthn_challenges, auth_events"
  prose "There are no foreign keys, so the set is self-contained; restoring the database restores identity. Runbook content is unaffected by a database restore."
step: "Health and readiness" section=true
  prose "`GET /healthz` returns `200 ok`, unauthenticated, on an identity-on instance too: wire container or load-balancer probes to it."
step: "Version" section=true
  prose "`runbooks --version` prints the build stamp: `runbooks vX.Y.Z` for a tagged build, the short commit otherwise. Tagged images also carry `org.opencontainers.image.version` and `…revision` labels:"
  code lang="bash" label="" body="docker inspect --format '{{index .Config.Labels \"org.opencontainers.image.version\"}}' \\\n  ghcr.io/runbooks-help/runbooks:<tag>"
===== ../docs/operations/identity.md =====
intro:
  prose "Runbooks can require named users, so that reads are gated and writes are attributed to a person. It is **off by default**: with no identity database configured the app is public and the sync API is gated by `GITSYNC_API_TOKEN`."
  prose "Sign-in is by **passkey** (WebAuthn): no password, no shared secret, no email. Registration is invite-only, and the first admin is created with a bootstrap token."
step: "Enabling" section=true
  table headers=["Var" "Meaning" "Default"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_DB_DRIVER`" "`sqlite`, `mysql` or `postgres`" "_(unset, identity off)_"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_DB_DSN`" "DSN / SQLite file path" "`file:./data/runbooks.db` (sqlite only)"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_PUBLIC_URL`" "Scheme + host; derives the WebAuthn RP ID and origin" "required when on"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN`" "Guards `/setup` for the first admin" "generated and logged once"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_RECOVERY_TOKEN`" "Break-glass re-enrolment for the last admin" "_(unset, `/recovery` disabled)_"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_SESSION_TTL` / `IDENTITY_SESSION_IDLE`" "Absolute / idle session lifetime" "`720h` / `168h`"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_SECURE_COOKIES`" "Cookie `Secure` flag" "`true`"]
  prose "`IDENTITY_PUBLIC_URL` is effectively immutable: changing the host after passkeys exist invalidates every credential."
  prose "Open `/setup` and present the bootstrap token to enrol the first admin; from there, `/admin` issues single-use invite links (`/invite/<token>`). With identity on, every page (including each runbook) redirects to `/login` when unauthenticated."
step: "Delegating to an upstream proxy" section=true
  prose "Instead of passkeys, the app can trust an identity asserted by a reverse proxy or SSO gateway in front of it:"
  table headers=["Var" "Meaning" "Default"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_TRUST_PROXY_AUTH`" "Trust the upstream identity header" "`false`"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_PROXY_USER_HEADER`" "Header carrying the login/email" "`Auth-Request-Email`"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_PROXY_NAME_HEADER`" "Header carrying the display name" "_(unset)_"]
  prose "On first sight the asserted identity is provisioned as a `member`; admin is never granted from a header. A request is resolved from the session cookie first, then the header, so a proxied request is authenticated without a login."
  notice variant="warn" message="Only enable this when the instance is **unreachable except through the proxy**, which must set and strip the header. A directly reachable instance is an impersonation hole: anyone who can reach it can set the header themselves."
  heading "Tailscale Serve"
  prose "`tailscale serve` injects `Tailscale-User-Login` and `Tailscale-User-Name` on requests it proxies, so a Tailnet-fronted instance maps directly onto proxy delegation:"
  code lang="bash" label="" body="IDENTITY_DB_DRIVER=sqlite \\\nIDENTITY_DB_DSN=file:./data/runbooks.db \\\nIDENTITY_PUBLIC_URL=https://runbooks.example.ts.net \\\nIDENTITY_TRUST_PROXY_AUTH=true \\\nIDENTITY_PROXY_USER_HEADER=Tailscale-User-Login \\\nIDENTITY_PROXY_NAME_HEADER=Tailscale-User-Name \\\n  ./runbooks"
  prose "with the app listening on loopback and `tailscale serve` in front of it. Caveats:"
  list ordered=false
    item "Serve omits the identity headers for **tagged devices** and for **Funnel** traffic, so those requests are anonymous."
    item "Non-ASCII values may be RFC 2047 \"Q\"-encoded (`=?utf-8?q?...?=`)."
    item "Behind a Kubernetes L7 operator `Ingress`, the proxy forwards to a cluster-reachable Service rather than localhost, so the equivalent of \"loopback only\" is a NetworkPolicy admitting only the ingress proxy pods to the app port."
step: "Attribution" section=true
  prose "With identity on, `/api/git-sync/v1` requires an authenticated session (or proxy assertion), and the commit author is the signed-in user: name and email from the user record. `GITSYNC_API_TOKEN` remains the non-human automation fallback for CI and scripts. Runbook acknowledgements are recorded against the authenticated user."
===== ../docs/reference/configuration.md =====
intro:
  prose "Everything is an environment variable; there is no config file. The defaults are what the container runs with."
step: "Server" section=true
  table headers=["Var" "Default" "Meaning"]
    row ["`PORT`" "`8090`" "HTTP listen port."]
    row ["`STYLEGUIDE_ENABLED`" "`false`" "Serve the design-system reference at `/styleguide` and `/styleguide/llms`."]
    row ["`PUBLIC_URL`" "_(unset)_" "Absolute site base, e.g. `https://runbooks.help` (trailing slash stripped). Enables canonical/OpenGraph URLs and `/sitemap.xml`."]
    row ["`SITE_DESCRIPTION`" "_(unset)_" "Default `<meta name=\"description\">` for pages without their own."]
  prose "`/healthz` is always served, unauthenticated, and returns `200 ok`."
step: "Content" section=true
  table headers=["Var" "Default" "Meaning"]
    row ["`CONTENT_DIR`" "`content`" "Directory the runbooks are read from when `CONTENT_SOURCE=local`. The image ships an empty one; mount your own."]
    row ["`CONTENT_SOURCE`" "`local`" "`local` (read `CONTENT_DIR`) or `git` (clone the remote into a cache and read it)."]
    row ["`CONTENT_GIT_PATH`" "`.`" "Directory within the repo to read when `CONTENT_SOURCE=git`."]
    row ["`CONTENT_GIT_CACHE`" "`data/content`" "Where the git clone lives; reused across restarts."]
    row ["`CONTENT_REFRESH_TOKEN`" "_(unset)_" "Bearer token for `POST /api/content/v1/refresh` when identity is off; unset disables the endpoint (it is admin-only when identity is on)."]
    row ["`CONTENT_REFRESH_INTERVAL`" "_(unset)_" "Opt-in background refresh, e.g. `5m`. Go duration; unset or `0` is off. Values below `1m` are rejected at startup. Applies to any source (`local` re-read, `git` fetch first)."]
  prose "With `CONTENT_SOURCE=git`, the remote and credential come from the `GITSYNC_*` variables below: content at the repo root, notes records in `GITSYNC_BASE_PATH`, which the content walk skips."
step: "Identity" section=true
  prose "Identity is **off** until `IDENTITY_DB_DRIVER` is set; with no driver the app is public as before."
  table headers=["Var" "Default" "Meaning"]
    row ["`IDENTITY_DB_DRIVER`" "_(unset)_" "`sqlite`, `mysql` or `postgres`. Unset = identity off."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_DB_DSN`" "`file:./data/runbooks.db` (sqlite)" "Driver DSN or SQLite file path."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_PUBLIC_URL`" "_(required when on)_" "Scheme + host; derives the WebAuthn RP ID and origin. Effectively immutable once passkeys exist."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN`" "_(generated and logged once)_" "Guards `/setup` for the first admin."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_RECOVERY_TOKEN`" "_(unset)_" "Break-glass re-enrolment for the sole admin; `/recovery` is disabled when unset."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_TRUST_PROXY_AUTH`" "`false`" "Trust an identity asserted by an upstream proxy/SSO gateway. Only safe when the app is unreachable except through that proxy."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_PROXY_USER_HEADER`" "`Auth-Request-Email`" "Header carrying the login/email."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_PROXY_NAME_HEADER`" "_(unset)_" "Header carrying the display name."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_SECURE_COOKIES`" "`true`" "Cookie `Secure` flag; relax only for local HTTP."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_SESSION_TTL`" "`720h`" "Absolute session lifetime."]
    row ["`IDENTITY_SESSION_IDLE`" "`168h`" "Idle session lifetime."]
  prose "`IDENTITY_BOOTSTRAP_TOKEN` is ignored once an admin exists. There is no API to rotate the bootstrap or recovery tokens: change the value and restart."
step: "Git sync" section=true
  prose "Sync is enabled only when a repo **and** a credential **and** an endpoint auth are configured. The endpoint auth is a user session when identity is on (an unauthenticated request is refused), or `GITSYNC_API_TOKEN` when identity is off."
  prose "A credential is an HTTPS token (`GITSYNC_TOKEN`), an explicit SSH private key (`GITSYNC_SSH_KEY`), or, for an `ssh://` or scp-style remote, the ambient SSH agent. With no key and no token, `go-git` authenticates SSH remotes through `$SSH_AUTH_SOCK` (ssh-agent, 1Password, …), so the common local setup needs no credential variable at all. Set `GITSYNC_SSH_KEY` only where there is no agent: a deployment or CI job that mounts a key and points the variable at its path. See [docs/operations/deployment.md](../operations/deployment.md#ssh-credentials) for the container recipe."
  table headers=["Var" "Default" "Meaning"]
    row ["`GITSYNC_REPO`" "_(unset)_" "Remote URL: any host, HTTPS or SSH. Unset disables sync."]
    row ["`GITSYNC_BRANCH`" "`main`" "Target branch."]
    row ["`GITSYNC_BASE_PATH`" "`runbook_runs`" "Directory prefix for records (skipped by the content walk when it sits inside the content tree)."]
    row ["`GITSYNC_AUTHOR_NAME`" "_(required)_" "Fallback commit identity, when the user has no email."]
    row ["`GITSYNC_AUTHOR_EMAIL`" "_(required)_" "Fallback commit email."]
    row ["`GITSYNC_USERNAME`" "`oauth2`" "HTTPS basic-auth username (token as password)."]
    row ["`GITSYNC_TOKEN`" "_(unset)_" "HTTPS access token."]
    row ["`GITSYNC_SSH_KEY`" "_(unset)_" "Private key path for SSH remotes; omit to use the ambient SSH agent."]
    row ["`GITSYNC_API_TOKEN`" "_(unset)_" "Shared bearer that gates the sync endpoint, for CI/automation and for identity-off deployments."]
  prose "Each sync writes a new, immutable snapshot directory under the base path, `<GITSYNC_BASE_PATH>/<YYYY-MM-DD>T<HHMMSSZ>-<slug>/` (UTC), so every sync is preserved; a re-sync with no changes is skipped."
  prose "With identity on, a signed-in user's commit is authored as that user (`DisplayName <Email>`); `GITSYNC_AUTHOR_*` applies only when the user has no email, and `GITSYNC_API_TOKEN` is the non-human fallback."
  prose "The git transport is `go-git` (pure Go); no system `git` binary is needed. SSH remotes are verified against `known_hosts` (`SSH_KNOWN_HOSTS`, then `~/.ssh/known_hosts` / `/etc/ssh/ssh_known_hosts`); there is no `accept-new`, so the host key must already be present."
